In a discussion about a report on agriculture impacts, James sought an extended section detailing climate impacts on agriculture. The initial text highlighted agriculture's vulnerability to climate risks and tropospheric ozone, noting that avoided damages by 2050 are under 0.1% of GDP. In response, I provided an expanded paragraph that explained the methodology from the Global Methane Assessment, emphasizing that the primary factors considered are temperature and ozone exposure. I clarified that while the empirical crop model reflects these key drivers, it does not directly account for impacts from drought or flooding, though changes in these factors may still indirectly influence agricultural risks. This addressed a reviewer question on the scope of climate impacts included in the assessment.
